./LOGBOOK.md:334:- FINDING: installed products settle it in **neither** direction. Re-read on this Mac: Surfshark ships the `/Library/Keychains/` exception at `LSMinimumSystemVersion` **10.15**, bundled with `/private/var/db/mds/` and a third path; PureVPN's two providers carry `keychain-access-groups` at **10.15**; Tailscale's carries neither at **12.4**. Support ranges that wide isolate nothing about macOS 15.0. K1's clause runs **both ways** — a key present in a shipping bundle is not proof the mechanism functions, and a key absent from one is not proof it is unnecessary.
./LOGBOOK.md:424:- SCOPE: `PlatformVPNIdentity` (`Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/VPNManagerRepository.swift:17`) declares `appGroupIdentifier` as a non-optional `String` for both platforms — after G4 there is no macOS value to supply. Recorded for `TASK-260715-1tzaed`, not fixed; `production(for:)` still throws so nothing is broken today. `.spec/architecture.md:111` joins `security-claims.md` and threat-model M-02 on the spec-amendment list.
./LOGBOOK.md:429:- DECISION: Revocation is now specified as a **five-state table**, not a yes/no. Synchronous when the configuration is installed and enabled (running or not — the send launches it); unavailable and deferred to a start-time sweep when the configuration is disabled (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationDisabled`), invalid/unapproved (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationInvalid`), or when no host is running at all. Seeding moved out of `startTunnel` entirely, so the credential is normally at rest before any start; `startTunnel` is now read-only and fails fast with `credentialNotProvisioned`. The start-time sweep survives as crash-recovery defence, not as the deletion path.
./LOGBOOK.md:432:- FINDING: Do not hard-code `/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`. `SecKeychainCopyDomainDefault(kSecPreferencesDomainSystem, &kc)` returns `OSStatus=0` and resolves to exactly that path from an ordinary user context (uid 502), and `SecKeychainCopyDomainSearchList` for the system domain has **count=1** — physically confirming TN3137's *"in the system context the search list includes just the System keychain"*. The fix is behaviour-preserving, so there was never a reason to pin the literal. The whole `SecKeychain`/`SecAccess` surface is `API_DEPRECATED(… macos(10.2, 10.10))` yet still ships in the 26.5 SDK and remains Apple's only documented daemon option.
./LOGBOOK.md:438:- FINDING: The decisive fact is physically readable on this Mac. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` (macOS 26.5/25F71, sha256 `2e711078…`): `(when (entitlement "com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension") (allow file-read* file-write* (subpath "/Library/Keychains")))`. An NE-entitled process already has System keychain access from the base App Sandbox. Lines 655-656 grant the `securityd`/`SecurityServer` Mach lookups to every sandboxed process. **The selected transport costs zero entitlements.**
./LOGBOOK.md:440:- ANOMALY: Second instance of the pattern logged at 0755 — **shipping entitlements are not evidence of requirement**. Surfshark's provider ships a `/Library/Keychains/` temporary exception the current sandbox profile makes redundant; Surfshark and Tailscale both ship `/private/var/db/mds/` exceptions dating to a Catalina-era bug fixed in Big Sur (forums/thread/660123), which `application.sb:544-546` already covers for uid 0; and PureVPN's provider carries `keychain-access-groups` that Facts 1-2 say cannot bridge the user boundary. Three shipping products, three stale or inert entitlements.
./LOGBOOK.md:445:- BLOCKED: Nothing. Named limitation instead — `sudo -n true` exit 1 in a non-interactive session, so **no code was run as root**. Checks V1–V3 (V1 System keychain write from the signed sandboxed provider, V2 root group-container path, V3 system start with the app not running) were appended to `TASK-260715-9yp8to` rather than creating a duplicate physical-Mac node (ADR-028a). r2 added **V4/V5** to the same node.
./LOGBOOK.md:457:- FINDING: Verified against three shipping Developer-ID packet-tunnel products installed on this Mac — Tailscale, PureVPN, Surfshark. All three carry the suffixed value on **both** the containing app and the embedded sysex, confirming the Developer ID half. No development-signed counterpart exists locally, so the development half rests on the DTS statement and is re-checked by assertion A3 at first signed build.
./LOGBOOK.md:461:- FINDING: Read `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` off three shipping Developer-ID host/provider pairs on this Mac: hosts `false` in all three (Tailscale, PureVPN, Surfshark), providers `true` in all three. Apple requires the **provider system extension** to be sandboxed; the containing app is not covered by that rule for direct distribution. r1 attributed both to a single Apple requirement.
./LOGBOOK.md:469:- FINDING: The r1 D-2 rationale ("the entitlement would be non-functional") was too strong. PureVPN's root sysexes carry `keychain-access-groups` outright and Surfshark's carries a `/Library/Keychains/` path exception — i.e. the **file-based System keychain** works from root. What fails is specifically the *Data Protection* Keychain sharing the spec asks for. The row stays withheld because its purpose is undetermined until `7ii1xz` picks a transport, not because it is inert.
./LOGBOOK.md:478:- BLOCKER: `.spec/security-privacy.md` requires private keys and passphrases to live in the Data Protection Keychain "shared only through the minimum keychain access group required by the app and its packet tunnel extension" (also `.spec/threat-model.md` DF-02/M-02). On macOS that is unsatisfiable. Developer ID direct distribution forces the provider to be a **system extension** (TN3134, `.spec/platform-distribution.md`), a sysex runs **as root** outside a user context, the Data Protection Keychain is available only to code in a user context, and keychain access groups share items between programs running as the **same** user. The host runs as the logged-in user. iOS is unaffected — its appex runs as the same user as its host.
./LOGBOOK.md:486:- DECISION: One bundle identifier per target across every build configuration — no `.debug`/`.dev` variants. macOS system-extension approval is keyed to Team ID + extension bundle identifier, so suffixing multiplies physical approval friction on Holds H2/H3, and `9yp8to` must exercise the identity that ships. Environment is expressed by signing identity class, profile class, hardened runtime, and `get-task-allow`. The single exception is the probe family, which exists so throwaway sysex approvals never burn the production identity.
./LOGBOOK.md:517:- DECISION: Ceremony C1 split. C1 is permission grants only (Keychain unlock, portal auth, identifier/profile authorization, notarytool profile, Sparkle keygen). The probe build `1r0fxv` and its independent review sit **outside** it, and Approval A1 (one click on the probe's system-VPN dialog) is a separate later interaction. The previous version claimed a human could remain present through a producer→reviewer cycle.
./LOGBOOK.md:526:- DECISION: Owner-approved strategy encoded in `.spec/decisions.md` as ADR-023 (M0 SSH viability scope, Option A, with the four deferred semantics named and owned by TASK-260728-3cveay) and ADR-024 (macOS-only physical target, iOS deferred, Linux CI nonblocking, Relux Works signing/notary available). ADR-013 re-scoped from a blocking prototype gate to a release-path gate; ADR-014 moved Open → Accepted with libssh2 as the primary engine.
./LOGBOOK.md:529:- FINDING: The macOS path was silently gated on iOS through five non-obvious edges — `1idq8c`, `1lmmri`, `1uxx3i`, and `sbrrp7` all waited on `33oofa` (iOS targets), and `3lab1f` waited on `2hiabd` (iOS provider adapter). Those four alone blocked every macOS UI task, the macOS release archive, and all macOS physical provider validation. Deferring a platform is not just skipping its tasks; the cross-platform "shared" tasks have to be re-rooted too.
./LOGBOOK.md:530:- DECISION: Ceremony C1 deliberately includes the Gate P0 system-VPN approval click. The disposable probe builds in minutes from profiles created minutes earlier, so it stays one sitting. Hold H2 (real app, different bundle identifier) cannot be folded in and remains the next human boundary after 202 autonomous tasks.
./LOGBOOK.md:586:- GATE: DNS candidate limits remain non-authoritative with `productionAuthorization.permitted=false`. Production use still requires accepted selected-SSH `direct-tcpip` timing/footprint/cleanup evidence (`TASK-260715-1gjxer`), the ADR-009 residual DNS component budget (`TASK-260715-1pn983`), physical-provider startup/footprint rows, and independent review.
./LOGBOOK.md:587:- BOARD: The placeholder M3 physical-validation story and manual ratification task were refined with exact macOS-first scope, acceptance criteria, privacy boundaries, and red/unavailable preservation. The protocol, machine schema, and activity diagram are linked as task-scoped outcomes and downstream preconditions.
./LOGBOOK.md:592:- PLATFORM: Apple TN3134 requires the directly distributed macOS packet tunnel to be a system extension. Sparkle host replacement/relaunch does not activate the new provider; the host must stop the tunnel, relaunch, submit a separate replacement request, surface approval/restart state, and avoid automatic reconnect. Exact OS behavior remains a physical gate under `TASK-260715-1r48pc` and `TASK-260715-2aessv`.
./LOGBOOK.md:597:- DECISION: Security copy now carries stable claim IDs and one of four evidence boundaries: implemented component, contract implemented/integration gated, accepted design/release gated, or planned/unverified. No positive system-VPN claim is authorized as a verified release claim until candidate-specific composition and physical evidence is accepted.
./LOGBOOK.md:599:- RESIDUAL: Production SSH selection (`TASK-260715-1gjxer`), DNS runtime authorization (`TASK-260721-3miqh4`), physical route/DNS evidence, credentials/entitlements, relay composition, update/release controls, and independent candidate review remain explicit evidence gates.
./LOGBOOK.md:641:- ACTIVITY: New associations and socketless reservations retain one bounded initial idle lifetime, but `Reserve`, `EnsureFamilies`, and `EnsureTokenFamilies` no longer touch an existing incarnation. The registry rearms activity only when the serialized socket callback reports a successful outbound send or received datagram. Deterministic injected-clock tests assert exact deadlines and physical timer-arm epochs across numeric and resolved-domain IPv4-to-IPv6 family attachment: success refreshes once; `EAGAIN`, `ENOBUFS`, resolver failure, caller cancellation, and terminal socket errors do not refresh before survival or cleanup.
./LOGBOOK.md:670:- REWORK: `EnsureFamilies` now reserves the complete canonical IPv4/IPv6 family set before opening either descriptor. A failure closes every staged descriptor and any socket already owned by that association exactly once, then restores association, socket, logical timer, event, and physical timer state to baseline. Lazy single-family admission remains available, but a failed later family completion also retires the partial association.
./LOGBOOK.md:671:- RACE EVIDENCE: A private owner-loop seam submits an explicit obsolete timer arm after rearm and proves the arm counter increments without changing the live association or its one current logical/physical timer. Barrier-controlled tests repeat descriptor-use and activity races against local close, remote close, expiry, generation replacement, session close, session loss, process termination, and parent cancellation; each path proves its permitted linearization, stale token rejection or terminal owner closure, exactly-once descriptor close, and baseline restoration.
./LOGBOOK.md:676:- RESOURCE POLICY: Association, family-specific socket, logical timer, and pending-close credits are checked before descriptor creation and report finite aggregate counters. One indexed deadline heap and one physical monotonic timer bound timer/task state; expiry events consume reserved pending-close credit until observed. IPv4 and IPv6 descriptors are created lazily and closed exactly once across normal, partial-failure, expiry, session, process, and cancellation paths.
./LOGBOOK.md:725:- AUTHORITY: The canonical artifact class is `non-authoritative-candidate`; `productionAuthorization.permitted` and `adr022MayAdvanceToAccepted` remain false. Exact blockers are `TASK-260715-1gjxer` and `TASK-260715-1pn983`, and selected-engine/physical provider rows remain a declared later external gate. No production DNS runtime or candidate numeric value changed.
./LOGBOOK.md:732:- STOP-LINE: The 4/8 MiB and timing figures are non-authoritative candidates. ADR-014 lacks accepted selected-SSH `direct-tcpip` rows (`TASK-260715-1gjxer`), and ADR-009 lacks an accepted residual DNS component ledger (`TASK-260715-1pn983`). Machine policy sets `productionAuthorization.permitted=false`; ADR-022 and all downstream consumers remain blocked pending those inputs, physical provider rows, and independent review.
./LOGBOOK.md:737:- MEASUREMENT: Three main fixture runs plus three isolated default/hard memory runs passed IPv4/IPv6/dual/unreachable/malformed/stall/concurrent-failure/cancellation/M2 semantics, zero resolver calls, byte-for-byte allocation, zero FD delta, and zero tracked live bytes. Maximum isolated incremental physical footprint was 3,653,656/7,749,656 bytes. The controlled loopback results validate mechanics and bounds, not production SSH latency.
./LOGBOOK.md:738:- REVIEW/REVALIDATION: ADR-022 remains Proposed and every downstream consumer remains blocked pending independent architecture acceptance. Selected-SSH, physical-iPhone, HEV/OS/toolchain, relay, diagnostics, and policy drift are explicit revalidation triggers. Evidence: `.research/260721_task-260721-3miqh4-dns-runtime-policy-v1.md`, machine vectors and raw summaries under `.research/`, harness `scripts/dns-policy-evidence.py`.
./LOGBOOK.md:742:- DECISION: ADR-022 proposes one required ordered per-profile `dnsResolver` with a non-empty list of canonical numeric IPv4/IPv6 `dns53` endpoints and per-endpoint port default 53. There is no product/public-vendor default, hostname endpoint, physical resolver inheritance, exit-host shell discovery, DoH, or fake-DNS baseline. The earlier one-to-four endpoint ceiling and 5/5/15/10-second, 32/128-query values had no accepted evidence and are removed. New TASK-260721-3miqh4 owns measured endpoint/message/byte/capacity/timing defaults and hard caps; schema, transport, relay, and M4 consumers are blocked on its independently accepted `DNSRuntimePolicyV1`.
./LOGBOOK.md:772:### 0535 — Host VPN production start errors are normalized at both Apple seams (TASK-260715-1rsqrh)
./LOGBOOK.md:773:- FIX: Both iOS and macOS `VPNHostSession` adapters now catch synchronous `NETunnelProviderSession.startTunnel(options:)` errors and translate them through the existing `VPNPreferencePlatformError` model before crossing into `VPNSessionController`. Public `NEVPNError.configurationInvalid`, `.configurationDisabled`, and `.connectionFailed` retain their stable results; every other `NSError` retains its original domain and integer code.
./LOGBOOK.md:774:- INTEGRATION: A freshly loaded exact-owned but disabled manager correctly failed inside `OwnedVPNManagerRepository`, but the controller initially leaked the repository error type. The start preflight now normalizes that one repository result to `VPNSessionControllerError.configurationDisabled` without invoking the system start API or duplicating ownership logic.
./LOGBOOK.md:778:### 0526 — Host VPN controller review requests production error-translation rework (TASK-260715-1rsqrh)
./LOGBOOK.md:780:- REGRESSION: `IOSVPNHostSession.startTunnel` and `MacOSVPNHostSession.startTunnel` forward the public `NETunnelProviderSession.startTunnel(options:)` thrown `NSError` unchanged, while `VPNSessionController.performStart` maps only `VPNPreferencePlatformError` / `VPNPlatformError` and otherwise erases the real domain/code to reflected type plus code 0. The unit test injects a pre-normalized fake error, so the production adapter path is untested and cannot satisfy the accepted exact synchronous `NEVPNError` mapping.
./LOGBOOK.md:783:### 0511 — Host VPN control keeps system and provider authorities independent (TASK-260715-1rsqrh)
./LOGBOOK.md:784:- DECISION: `OwnedVPNManagerRepository` remains the only ownership/schema authority and now hands out a session only from a freshly loaded exact-owned current manager. `VPNSessionController` retains no manager cache or forwarding state; iOS/macOS wrappers retain the fresh manager only to keep its exact public `NETunnelProviderSession` alive for the command/observer lifetime.
./LOGBOOK.md:787:- ERROR CONTRACT: All 19 public `NEVPNConnectionError` codes, provider codes 1001–1009, unknown domains/codes, provider unknown codes, nil errors, and timeout/cancellation unavailability map to stable finite reasons while system status remains authoritative.
./LOGBOOK.md:790:### 0442 — Apple VPN terminal observation registers before authoritative status (TASK-260715-15vkvz)
./LOGBOOK.md:791:- REGRESSION: Both NetworkExtension adapters read `NEVPNConnection.status` before installing `NEVPNStatusDidChange`, so a terminal transition in that gap could be lost and an already-stopped session could falsely reach the 15-second `stopTimedOut` path.
./LOGBOOK.md:796:### 0421 — VPN preference operations are non-reentrant and NSNumber versions are exact (TASK-260715-15vkvz)
./LOGBOOK.md:802:### 0405 — VPN manager rework preserves all future versions and fresh authority (TASK-260715-15vkvz)
./LOGBOOK.md:808:### 0351 — Owned VPN preferences are shared policy with thin platform clients (TASK-260715-15vkvz)
./LOGBOOK.md:810:- SAFETY: Ensure never enables; the explicit enable result names the OS-level possibility that another enterprise VPN is disabled. Duplicate repair removes every freshly revalidated current-schema inactive exact-owned manager, reloads after each removal, proves zero, then recreates one. Unmarked, unrelated, wrong-type, corrupt duplicate, active duplicate, and future-owned managers are zero-write paths.
./LOGBOOK.md:811:- IDENTITY: The complete host/provider/App Group/Keychain `PlatformVPNIdentity` remains injectable for deterministic fixtures. Both production factories fail closed until `TASK-260715-ypo7yo` supplies accepted signed bindings; no guessed identifier landed.
./LOGBOOK.md:821:### 0245 — VPN lifecycle separates ownership, enablement, system state, and runtime capability (TASK-260715-1q4qhw)
./LOGBOOK.md:823:- PLATFORM FINDING: Apple limits `loadAllFromPreferences` to configurations created by the calling app, but enabling an enterprise VPN can cause the OS to disable another app's enterprise VPN. Therefore ordinary ensure preserves/creates a disabled configuration and explicit foreground user intent owns enable/save/reload. Relux never mutates the unrelated manager object and reports the system-level enable effect truthfully.
./LOGBOOK.md:945:- FINDING: Pinned HEV caps UDP-in-TCP copy buffers at `UDP_BUF_SIZE = 1500` (`hev-socks5-udp.c:26`); `recvmmsg_tcp` returns `-1` (stream-fatal, not a drop) when `datlen > 1500 − addrlen` (`hev-socks5-udp.c:203-206`). With ADR-020 keeping HEV unmodified, the relay→client reply path physically cannot carry a UDP payload above 1481 bytes (IPv6). This, plus unfragmented 1500-MTU egress, fixes `maxUDPPayload = 1472` as a v1 wire constant; raising it is an HEV-fork decision, not a protocol negotiation.
./LOGBOOK.md:980:- MILESTONE: BUG-260720-2zh86a accepted → done. `make validate-native` full Apple matrix no longer stops on the HEV/libssh2 deployment-target mismatch; packet-plane Apple builds and physical validation (12x6oq) unblocked on this axis.
./LOGBOOK.md:1127:- DECISION: ADR-014 remains open. Both candidates retain identical host-key, window, sustained 5 GiB rekey, scale (through 1,000 channels), cancellation, algorithm, extension-build, memory, and physical-size gates. Unknowns are named experiments in `.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md`; no missing capability was waived.
